{"parentUuid":"b9783144-41ef-418e-8415-4008cad808ca","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"57b92f92-bd90-4448-a169-4e54be734ab6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xbz7vT3vis2jqP6QQCzSN6","type":"tool_result","content":"# AGENTS.md\n\nProject: Flint\n\n## What this is\nFlint is Claude working as Eric's sport-science, performance, S&C and rehab thought\npartner. This repo builds the knowledge base behind that role. It catalogs Eric's\nDrive (his own material, TB's Training Info, Landon Evans, courses, papers), routes the\nhigh-value sources into Zotero and the SecondBrain vault, and writes per-discipline\nsynthesis notes that compare the sources against Eric's own method. It is a side-lane\nproject, outside the prioritization-slate cap. See `README.md` for the approach and\n`docs/PLAN.md` for the checklist.\n\n## Session routine\n- Start: run `git pull`, then read docs/STATUS.md before doing anything else.\n- End: update docs/STATUS.md (done, next, open questions), commit, and `git push`.\n- Never leave uncommitted work at the end of a session.\n\n## Conventions\n- Durable notes and decisions go in docs/, committed. Not in chat, not in cloud project docs.\n- Documents, data exports, and deliverables live on the primary storage drive (Google Drive 00_Primary_Storage_Drive), not in the repo. Files over 95 MB never go in git.\n- `data/` is gitignored except aggregate summaries. Regenerate the file-level manifest with `pwsh scripts/build_manifest.ps1`.\n- Codex workers read staged copies in `staging/`, never the Drive root. DexaProject, athlete snapshots, Catapult and PFF exports are never catalogued.\n- The ingest review gate has two stages.\n  1. Before atomizing, verify the source artifact by an independent route: page order, scanned versus text, transcripts checked against captions.\n  2. Before promoting, Codex reviews the staged entries against the staged sources, with instructions to falsify (`staging/review_<source>_codex.md`). Nothing reaches the vault without a GO or a GO AFTER FIXES that has been applied.\n- Library entries label every voice. The source author's statements are plain. Eric's judgments get \"Eric's review:\". The entry writer's own reasoning gets \"Inference:\". No unlabeled judgments. Every number carries a timestamp or page the reviewer can check, and a claim is only tagged \"confirmed in PDF\" if that PDF is staged. (Learned from the Harries review, 2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes, mostly for attribution.)\n- Writing: plain, direct, no em dashes. Documents use the Baylor design system.\n--- STATUS\n# STATUS\n\nRead this first. Update it before ending every session.\n\n## Current state (2026-09-27, end of day 2)\n- **In the vault (SecondBrain `aacbccd`, pushed 2026-09-27):** 269 entries across 6 sources, all through the two-stage review gate, plus 66 reciprocal links. Staging was synced back from the vault (a promote dry run shows 0 changed).\n\n  | Book | Entries | Gate |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | Harries *On-Field RTP* (`rehab-and-return-to-play/on-field-rtp--harries`) | 37 (33 + batch 2) | 2 rounds; batch 2: 1 round |\n  | Old Bull (`performance-technology-and-leadership/old-bull-performance-course--old-bull`), new domain | 37 | 2 rounds |\n  | Issurin *Block Periodization* Ch1-3 (`block-periodization--issurin`) | 28 | Ch1-2: 2 rounds; Ch3: 1 round |\n  | Bondarchuk *Transfer of Training* I, Ch1-2 (`transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk`) | 20 | 2 rounds |\n  | *Supertraining* Ch1-3 (`supertraining--verkhoshansky`) | 113 | Ch1: 3 rounds; Ch2-3a: 2; Ch3b: 1 |\n  | Landon Evans *Elite Sports Performance* (`elite-sports-performance--evans`) | 34 | 2 rounds |\n\n- **Search index:** memsearch re-indexed 2026-09-27; spot checks return the new Issurin, Evans, Old Bull and Supertraining Ch3 entries as top hits.\n- *Supertraining* is atomized through Chapter 3 (113 entries). Resume at Ch4 (printed 201, PDF idx 212).\n- CFTS OCR done 2026-09-27: `staging/ocr/cfts--francis.md` (218 pages, 0 low-confidence, 12 blank), so F3 in the queue is unblocked. Zatsiorsky 2e OCR (`bash scripts/ocr_zatsiorsky_2e_chunks.sh`, skips finished chunks) and the CVASPS transcription were stopped 2026-09-27 by Claude Code because the laptop ran low on memory; nothing was saved. Restart them one at a time.\n- Branch `step1-triage` holds all of this (pushed). `main` needs the draft PR merged.\n\n## Next\n1. **Promotion is done.** For the next book (or a book already in the vault), repeat the recipe: `scripts/promote_to_vault.py` (dry run, then `--apply`), merge `_domain.md` by hand, `scripts/apply_reciprocal_links.py`, commit and push SecondBrain, `memsearch index`, then `scripts/sync_back_from_vault.py` and commit in Flint.\n2. Merge the `step1-triage` draft PR into `main`.\n3. Add Eric's annotation to the vault Issurin 2016 brief: its \"concentrated unidirectional\" model is Verkhoshansky's, not Issurin's A-T-R (pp. 6-7, 11).\n4. Missing ideas logged by reviewers (in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md` and `staging/review_*.md`): a next atomizer batch per book. Biggest: Supertraining 3.3.3 strength and speed; Issurin's five Ch1-2 ideas; Harries speed prescription (kept inside broken-stride).\n5. Continue the queue (`docs/QUEUE.md`): *Supertraining* Ch4-9, Bondarchuk Vol I Ch3, then Zatsiorsky, Francis (CFTS once OCR is done), other Verkhoshansky books.\n6. CVASPS transcript: once it exists, check Evans entries against it and raise matches from `untested` to `supported`.\n7. Jev pilot: build the state for the 200 files and write Claude's blind answer key.\n\n## Open questions\n- Should the Mike Golden library (1,245 files) get the same priority as TB and Landon?\n- Interview (when Eric has time): `docs/interview-prep.md`, 27 questions. New: slide U4 L1 p.16 (Evans) has \"no more than 4x per year\" body-composition testing, which may be the origin of the same rule in `method-baseline.md`.\n- Evans Verkhoshansky activation-levels entry: the slides contradict themselves on where the threshold of adaptability sits; both are recorded and the entry stays `untested` (the course ceiling) rather than `contested`. Eric's call.\n- Two-a-day entry (Issurin): confirm the current NCAA Division I preseason practice rule before using it for fall camp.\n\n## Answered\n- **How to edit this worktree** (open question from 2026-09-26): enter the existing worktree by path (`EnterWorktree` with `.claude/worktrees/step1-triage`). The local sources (`staging/books`, `staging/ocr`, `staging/audio`, `staging/sources`) exist only there.\n\n## Gotchas\n- **After promotion the vault copy is canonical.** Reciprocal links and `_domain.md` merges are written in the vault only, so staging is synced back from it (Next 1.5). When re-promoting a book that is already in the vault (Supertraining after Ch4, Harries batch 3), any file the dry run reports as \"changed\" that you did not edit in staging means investigate, not copy: `--apply` would overwrite the vault version and drop its links.\n- **Fixer logs can overstate.** One Bondarchuk fixer log claimed fixes that were not in the files. Round-2 reviewers check the files, and fixers paste grep output. Run the lint (`python scripts/lint_library.py staging/library/<domain>/<book>` for body em dashes, review-history words, confidence and sections; `scripts/promote_to_vault.py` dry run for links) before promoting.\n- **Section headers** use the vault form `**What it is** —`. Agents drifted to `: mandatory` and `- mandatory` suffixes; normalized 2026-09-27.\n- **Reviewer \"(verify)\" citations** come from model memory. Never insert one unless the vault or the paper confirms it.\n- **Background jobs die with the session.** Run long OCR in `--pages` chunks to separate files (Zatsiorsky 2e is 247 pages, about 3 hours) so a death loses one chunk. CFTS: 218 pages, about 2.5 hours.\n- **Page numbers.** The ingest-book extractor's `--pages` is inclusive and has a `viewer_page` field (claude-skills PR #9). Entries cite the 0-based `page` (= `viewer_page` minus 1). The Mac needs `git pull` in `~/.claude/skills`, then the sync script.\n- **Codex runs on Eric's ChatGPT plan cap.** When capped, reviews use fresh Opus agents with `staging/review_book_prompt.md`.\n- **Every atomizer follows `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`**, which now carries the 2026-09-27 lessons.\n- **`staging/` tracking.** `library/`, `review_*.md`, `*_prompt.md`, `fixes_*.md`, `reciprocal_links_*.md` and `_scope_*.txt` are committed. Books, audio, sources and OCR stay local.\n- **`gws` output.** It prints \"Using keyring backend\" on stdout, and scripts filter that line out.\n- **TB attribution.** TB is shortcut-linked inside AP & Sports Science. The manifest skips shortcuts to other configured roots so TB keeps its own attribution.\n- **Triage patterns.** The rules match underscores as spaces, so folder-name patterns use `.` in place of `_`.\n--- handoffs\n2026-09-27-flint-day2.md\n2026-09-26-flint-day1.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"dee75562-e19d-407a-b380-94640603e38e","timestamp":"2026-09-28T13:32:01.202Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# AGENTS.md\n\nProject: Flint\n\n## What this is\nFlint is Claude working as Eric's sport-science, performance, S&C and rehab thought\npartner. This repo builds the knowledge base behind that role. It catalogs Eric's\nDrive (his own material, TB's Training Info, Landon Evans, courses, papers), routes the\nhigh-value sources into Zotero and the SecondBrain vault, and writes per-discipline\nsynthesis notes that compare the sources against Eric's own method. It is a side-lane\nproject, outside the prioritization-slate cap. See `README.md` for the approach and\n`docs/PLAN.md` for the checklist.\n\n## Session routine\n- Start: run `git pull`, then read docs/STATUS.md before doing anything else.\n- End: update docs/STATUS.md (done, next, open questions), commit, and `git push`.\n- Never leave uncommitted work at the end of a session.\n\n## Conventions\n- Durable notes and decisions go in docs/, committed. Not in chat, not in cloud project docs.\n- Documents, data exports, and deliverables live on the primary storage drive (Google Drive 00_Primary_Storage_Drive), not in the repo. Files over 95 MB never go in git.\n- `data/` is gitignored except aggregate summaries. Regenerate the file-level manifest with `pwsh scripts/build_manifest.ps1`.\n- Codex workers read staged copies in `staging/`, never the Drive root. DexaProject, athlete snapshots, Catapult and PFF exports are never catalogued.\n- The ingest review gate has two stages.\n  1. Before atomizing, verify the source artifact by an independent route: page order, scanned versus text, transcripts checked against captions.\n  2. Before promoting, Codex reviews the staged entries against the staged sources, with instructions to falsify (`staging/review_<source>_codex.md`). Nothing reaches the vault without a GO or a GO AFTER FIXES that has been applied.\n- Library entries label every voice. The source author's statements are plain. Eric's judgments get \"Eric's review:\". The entry writer's own reasoning gets \"Inference:\". No unlabeled judgments. Every number carries a timestamp or page the reviewer can check, and a claim is only tagged \"confirmed in PDF\" if that PDF is staged. (Learned from the Harries review, 2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes, mostly for attribution.)\n- Writing: plain, direct, no em dashes. Documents use the Baylor design system.\n--- STATUS\n# STATUS\n\nRead this first. Update it before ending every session.\n\n## Current state (2026-09-27, end of day 2)\n- **In the vault (SecondBrain `aacbccd`, pushed 2026-09-27):** 269 entries across 6 sources, all through the two-stage review gate, plus 66 reciprocal links. Staging was synced back from the vault (a promote dry run shows 0 changed).\n\n  | Book | Entries | Gate |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | Harries *On-Field RTP* (`rehab-and-return-to-play/on-field-rtp--harries`) | 37 (33 + batch 2) | 2 rounds; batch 2: 1 round |\n  | Old Bull (`performance-technology-and-leadership/old-bull-performance-course--old-bull`), new domain | 37 | 2 rounds |\n  | Issurin *Block Periodization* Ch1-3 (`block-periodization--issurin`) | 28 | Ch1-2: 2 rounds; Ch3: 1 round |\n  | Bondarchuk *Transfer of Training* I, Ch1-2 (`transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk`) | 20 | 2 rounds |\n  | *Supertraining* Ch1-3 (`supertraining--verkhoshansky`) | 113 | Ch1: 3 rounds; Ch2-3a: 2; Ch3b: 1 |\n  | Landon Evans *Elite Sports Performance* (`elite-sports-performance--evans`) | 34 | 2 rounds |\n\n- **Search index:** memsearch re-indexed 2026-09-27; spot checks return the new Issurin, Evans, Old Bull and Supertraining Ch3 entries as top hits.\n- *Supertraining* is atomized through Chapter 3 (113 entries). Resume at Ch4 (printed 201, PDF idx 212).\n- CFTS OCR done 2026-09-27: `staging/ocr/cfts--francis.md` (218 pages, 0 low-confidence, 12 blank), so F3 in the queue is unblocked. Zatsiorsky 2e OCR (`bash scripts/ocr_zatsiorsky_2e_chunks.sh`, skips finished chunks) and the CVASPS transcription were stopped 2026-09-27 by Claude Code because the laptop ran low on memory; nothing was saved. Restart them one at a time.\n- Branch `step1-triage` holds all of this (pushed). `main` needs the draft PR merged.\n\n## Next\n1. **Promotion is done.** For the next book (or a book already in the vault), repeat the recipe: `scripts/promote_to_vault.py` (dry run, then `--apply`), merge `_domain.md` by hand, `scripts/apply_reciprocal_links.py`, commit and push SecondBrain, `memsearch index`, then `scripts/sync_back_from_vault.py` and commit in Flint.\n2. Merge the `step1-triage` draft PR into `main`.\n3. Add Eric's annotation to the vault Issurin 2016 brief: its \"concentrated unidirectional\" model is Verkhoshansky's, not Issurin's A-T-R (pp. 6-7, 11).\n4. Missing ideas logged by reviewers (in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md` and `staging/review_*.md`): a next atomizer batch per book. Biggest: Supertraining 3.3.3 strength and speed; Issurin's five Ch1-2 ideas; Harries speed prescription (kept inside broken-stride).\n5. Continue the queue (`docs/QUEUE.md`): *Supertraining* Ch4-9, Bondarchuk Vol I Ch3, then Zatsiorsky, Francis (CFTS once OCR is done), other Verkhoshansky books.\n6. CVASPS transcript: once it exists, check Evans entries against it and raise matches from `untested` to `supported`.\n7. Jev pilot: build the state for the 200 files and write Claude's blind answer key.\n\n## Open questions\n- Should the Mike Golden library (1,245 files) get the same priority as TB and Landon?\n- Interview (when Eric has time): `docs/interview-prep.md`, 27 questions. New: slide U4 L1 p.16 (Evans) has \"no more than 4x per year\" body-composition testing, which may be the origin of the same rule in `method-baseline.md`.\n- Evans Verkhoshansky activation-levels entry: the slides contradict themselves on where the threshold of adaptability sits; both are recorded and the entry stays `untested` (the course ceiling) rather than `contested`. Eric's call.\n- Two-a-day entry (Issurin): confirm the current NCAA Division I preseason practice rule before using it for fall camp.\n\n## Answered\n- **How to edit this worktree** (open question from 2026-09-26): enter the existing worktree by path (`EnterWorktree` with `.claude/worktrees/step1-triage`). The local sources (`staging/books`, `staging/ocr`, `staging/audio`, `staging/sources`) exist only there.\n\n## Gotchas\n- **After promotion the vault copy is canonical.** Reciprocal links and `_domain.md` merges are written in the vault only, so staging is synced back from it (Next 1.5). When re-promoting a book that is already in the vault (Supertraining after Ch4, Harries batch 3), any file the dry run reports as \"changed\" that you did not edit in staging means investigate, not copy: `--apply` would overwrite the vault version and drop its links.\n- **Fixer logs can overstate.** One Bondarchuk fixer log claimed fixes that were not in the files. Round-2 reviewers check the files, and fixers